**user**:
> I am writing a recommendation letter for a student. I have two previous letters that I am working with. One goes like this:
> ```
> To whom it may concern,
> 
> I am pleased to recommend Mohammad Taky Tahmid for [POSITION]. Tahmid is in the final semester of his PhD student in Environmental Economics at the University of Delware, where I act as his primary advisor, and he has been a steady and important contributor to several research efforts in our group. His work spans empirical climate–economy analysis, analysis of environmental pollution on health, and several applied projects involving climate, agricultural, and environmental data. He is one of the most broadly capable students I have worked with in recent years.
> Tahmid has taken on substantial responsibilities in three major funded projects. In the DNREC Information Gaps Analysis, he helped assemble and organize a large set of marine datasets used to support the state’s marine spatial planning work. This included cleaning, cataloging, and integrating nearly 300 datasets, which required close attention to detail and an understanding of how different physical and socioeconomic layers fit together. He created a geodatabase from this work in support of DNREC’s Ocean and Bay Plan. This work forms the basis of the paper “Data Gaps off the Delaware Coast: A Spatial Approach to Data Gaps for Marine Spatial Planning,” where he is the first author. 
> He is also a co-author on our ongoing paper on current economic losses from climate change. Tahmid led the development of the international trade spillover component, using input–output tables. This was a complicated part of the analysis, for which he self-organized his time studying a new area and performing analysis leading to an exciting new solution to the estimation of climate spill-overs. This paper is currently under revision at Nature.
> In support of the Climate and Clean Air Coalition’s (CCAC) Economic Assessment, Tahmid has been working on the PAGE integrated assessment model. He helped develop new models of future PM₂.₅ concentrations and implemented these into the PAGE model. He also helped me to integrate mortality risks from heat into this model. This work has required learning new tools and codebases, and he has done so effectively. This innovative assessment has been selected as a spotlight report by UNEP.
> Tahmid’s dissertation reflects the same range of skills.
> •	His first chapter combined empirical models of the global crop losses from climate change with simulations from three biophysical process agriculture models, to identify gaps and find ways to improve future projections of these losses.
> •	His second chapter examines the role of meals provided to farmer workers in Bangladesh, to study the risks these workers are exposed to and the drivers behind their wages.
> •	His third chapter examines how climatic shocks influence nutrition outcomes, through the channel of agricultural productivity.
> Across these chapters, he has assembled large datasets, adapted methods from the literature, and developed his own econometric specifications after working through several alternatives. His work is excellent and contributes to ongoing discussions about climate impacts on agriculture and welfare.
> Before joining UD, Tahmid taught at the Dhaka School of Economics and other institutions. Although he has not served as a TA here, he has been helpful to his classmates on many occasions. He often assists others with coding questions, talks through research ideas with them, and helps with data handling or general project setup. He has also been a reliable source of support for other members of our research group when they need help getting started on coding or analysis tasks.
> Tahmid has also been active in student organizations on campus. He currently serves as Treasurer of the Muslim Student Association and represented the School of Marine Science and Policy (SMSP) in the Graduate Student Government. He previously served on the SMSP Director’s Council as the student representative from the Newark campus and has participated in mentorship programs like i-Buddy for new international students. These activities have helped build community and support students across the UD campus.
> Overall, Tahmid has shown consistent progress and has taken on work that has been meaningful for our research group. He works independently, communicates clearly, and is reliable in completing tasks. I believe he would benefit from the support of this fellowship, and I am strongly recommending him for it.
> ```
> The jobs are mostly postdocs, where he is proposing a project. So, I'd like to also include material I have from the other letter:
> ```
> His project is “Heat exposure, productivity, and adaptation in Bangladesh rice mills.” Under the proposed project, he will collect repeated production and labor records from about 100 automated and semi-automated rice mills in northwestern Bangladesh during the hot season. By comparing hotter days within each region and cooler and hotter regions, he will be able to isolate the role of temperature on worker productivity. This information is relevant both to climate adaptation investment in Bangladesh and better understanding the risk of climate change and heat waves across the developing world. Labour productivity is one of the most economically significant impacts of climate change, but real-world evidence on it remains limited. In addition to the academic contribution, Tahmid’s work has the potential to feed directly into Bangladesh’s climate adaptation planning.
> ```
> 
> But I'm not sure how to organize this. Should the project proposal stuff go at the end, or does it need to go further up?
**assistant**:
> For postdoctoral applications, put the proposed project **near the beginning**—usually the second paragraph. Committees want to know both *what he proposes to do* and *why he is credible to do it*. Then use the rest of the letter as evidence for that credibility.
> 
> A strong order would be:
> 
> 1. **Opening endorsement and relationship**  
>    State that you are his primary advisor, his PhD stage, broad research strengths, and unambiguous recommendation.
> 
> 2. **Proposed postdoctoral project and significance**  
>    Briefly describe the rice-mill project, its design, why it addresses an important gap, and its policy relevance for Bangladesh. Keep this to one concise paragraph.
> 
> 3. **Why Tahmid can execute it**  
>    Connect the proposal to demonstrated skills: empirical climate–economy analysis, agricultural/labor questions in Bangladesh, large data collection and management, econometric design, and independent work.
> 
> 4. **Selected research accomplishments**  
>    Use the three funded projects, perhaps prioritizing the trade-spillovers paper and CCAC/PAGE work for technically demanding, high-profile evidence. DNREC can demonstrate data integration and project delivery.
> 
> 5. **Dissertation evidence**  
>    Summarize its coherence around climate, agriculture, labor, nutrition, and welfare—especially chapters that support the proposed work.
> 
> 6. **Collegiality, teaching/service, and closing endorsement**  
>    Keep these shorter for a research postdoc unless the position emphasizes teaching or community engagement.
> 
> You might introduce the project like this:
> 
> > Tahmid’s proposed project, “Heat Exposure, Productivity, and Adaptation in Bangladesh Rice Mills,” is a strong and timely extension of his broader research agenda on climate, agriculture, and welfare in Bangladesh. He proposes to collect repeated production and labor records from approximately 100 automated and semi-automated rice mills during the hot season. By leveraging variation in daily temperatures within regions and across cooler and hotter regions, the project will provide unusually valuable real-world evidence on how heat affects worker productivity and adaptation. The results would contribute to a major gap in the climate-impacts literature and could directly inform adaptation planning in Bangladesh.
> 
> Then explicitly transition: “His prior work makes him particularly well prepared to carry out this research.”
> 
> Also fix the opening wording to “in the final semester of his PhD in Environmental Economics at the University of Delaware.”
